State the accessor feature code uses to reach these, because that is exactly what `/audit-tokens`
flags the absence of: a raw duration literal is a finding precisely because a named token exists.

## Motion grammar

The rules for *which* motion applies *when*. This is the section that makes motion feel designed
rather than decorated.

A grammar answers questions like: what does an element arriving into view do, versus one changing in
place? Does a dismissal reverse its entrance, or exit differently? Which curve belongs to an
entrance and which to a morph?

<!-- DECIDE: your grammar. Be specific enough to settle an argument. "Motion should feel natural"
     cannot be violated and so cannot be enforced; "arriving elements ease out, in-place morphs
     ease in-out" can — and an audit can be written for it. -->

Where a rule exists because of a real finding rather than taste, say so. A rule with a story behind
it survives; one that reads as arbitrary gets overridden by the next person in a hurry.

## Known violations

